stream
OFIIICIAL USE ONLY
A trip to the Fort Knox Armor Center by one of the seniorparticipants resulted in the selection and loan to HERO by theArmor Center of 2L unpublished monographs, several publishedworks that had been difficult to obtain in this area, and 14after action reports, other unit reports, and miscellaneouspapers. This material was all examined, and much of it was founduseful for the study.The staff of the Historical services Division and of theHistories Division of the Office, Chief of Military History,made available a number of unpublished monographs that wene es-pecially helpful in filling in the German side of several actions.Especially valuable was the advice of these experienced historiansand researchers as to which materials were available elsewhere,and which simply wene nonexistent or not available in this country.The staff of the Docurnent Reference Library of the ResearchAnalysis Corporation made available, and in sevenal cases borrowedfor the Study participantS, vari-ous materials, especially classi-fied studies on the use of armor. These, while generally addingnothing to the data for specific operatlons, were valuable forgeneral background, including as they did such material as overallstatistics on tank losses by causative agent, theoretical work ontank roles, and efforts to derive desirable stnength ratios of op-posing tanks. Other similar rnaterial was borrowed for the study
endstream
