participants by SA/DOD. Mueh of this material is listed andbriefly discussed in Appendix B (contained in classified annex).About a dozen personal interviews wlth action participantswere also conducted by one of the study participants (ColonelCrosby). Although these could not, of course, yield numericaldata on strengths and losses, they brought out seve:ral otherwiseunrecorded and very significant factors in the battles (as, forinstance, the use of white phosphorus at Arracourt). Action par-ticipants were abl-e to suggest sources of data in some cases, andat least one action participant had personal records in his pos-session that did give some detailed numerical data.Published secondary sources were also used, the most usefulbeing the Office, Chief of Military History, volumes in theseries rlUS Army in World [,r/ar II .rfA good deal of research was also required to obtain in detailthe characteristics of the tanks that took part in the actionsstudied. A most valuable source for German World War II tanks wasa mirneographed British work, with photographs, 'rlllustrated Recordof German Army Equipment, 1939-1945,rtVol. III (M.I .10, The Wa::office, L947 ) Ient to HERO by RAC. B.H. Liddell-Hartrs book, TheTanks, contains less detailed charts of characteristics for WorldWar II tanks of al} nations except the Soviet Union. Armor: AHistorv of Mechanized Forces by R.M. Ogorkiewicz (New York:Praeger, 1960) provided some useful information on Soviet tanks,
