but no detaited data. Handout data sheets published by US ArmyOrdnance on US and foreign tanks were also used. Contemporary USintelligence reports on German tanks, found at the Federal RecordsCenter gave tank charactenistics as observed in combat and derivedfrom captured tanks. In general, the sources agreed closely. Achallenging research problem was the determination of which modelof the eight models of the German Pz.Kpfw. IV, for example, wasinvolved in a given action in which the sources report only 'rMarkIVs.tt Data on development and dates of introduction of the vari-ous models, given in ItIllustrated Record of German Army Equipmentrrand Liddell-Hart, together with slim clues in contemporary accountsof the actions, and other sources, were helpful in satisfactonilyresolving this problem.
Interpretation and Use of DataEach of the ten actions treated has been Dresented in apaper that includes the following pants:+'I. A narrative account of the action.2. An analysis of the action, with emphasis on the factorsdetermining success of the winning side.
