overall German attack (arong with Guderian), and had a better tankIoss ratio than the aggregate of all German panzer divisions. Thisstudy estirnates Rommelrs losses for this campaign at 72, whilelosses inflicted by him on the Alries were 161. rndices of effec-tiveness were thus 2.47 for Rommer and .4 for. the Arried units en-countered. The same determinants obtained in this portion of thecampaign as for the campaign as a who1e.A Group Franc South of the Somme.June 1940
This sma1l-unit action had a well-led and integrated Frenchcombat team launching a mobile counterattack from a defensive baseand inflicting disproportionate rosses on a German unit that hadthree times as many tanks but lacked reconnaissance or support.The French Group Franc consisted of 5 Sornua tanks heavily armoredfor the tlme and armed with a very powerful 47mm. gun, 5 panhardarmored cars armed with 25mm. guns, 2 47nm. antitank guns, 2 25mm.antitank guns, heavy machineguns, and a motorcycle reconnaissancecompany. The Germans had 14 Pz.Kpfw. rV tanks with 30mm. of armorand a short 75mm. gun. The German tanks were the most powerfulthey possessed at the time, yet the French 47mm. guns easily pene-tnated them, while the German 75mm. gun of that time was onlyrarely effective against the 40mm. armor of the Somua. The Germans,without support or prior neconnaissance, blundered into the welI-prepared and coondinated French defense and in fightlng their way
