Arracourt, 19-22 Septenber 1944This tank battle near Arracourt, France, was won by CombatCommand A of the US 4th Armored Divislon through superior leader-ship and tactics, despite a German numerical superiority of 5:3(in the medium tanks that actually fought the battle), and despitethe overall qualitative superiority of the German Mark IV (Pz.Kpfw.IV) and Mark V (Pz.Kpfw. V) tanks to the US M4. Responding to aGerman counterattack by the lltth and ll3th Panzer Brigades, theUS forces achieved a US-German loss ratio of 4:1 (gZ German tankslost to 2t US tanks lost). US leadership effectively used theprinciples of mass and maneuver, concentrating t{Elf tanks andlaunching repeated spoiling attacks, while the Germans wastedthei:: tanks in piecemeal commitments.The superior mobility of the US M4 on dry, firm ground, andits power-traverse, quickly turning turret, more than compensatedfon the greater penetration capability of the German tank armament--75mm. guns, 43, 48, and 70 calibers long. Low visibility due tofog prevented US air support that would otherwise have been avail-able, but the fog also allowed the Americans to close ao . Orrtanceat which their less powerfuJ- L/40 guns became effective. WelI-coordinated use of the highly effective tank destroyers and of ar-tillery support contributed to the success. US use of white phos-phorus as a first nound allowed quick adjustment of fire, forced
