The Sa.ar Campaiqn, 8 Novernber-7 December 1944Bad weather was the most influentiar factor in the saar cam-paign, November-December 1944. continuar rain made the groundnearly impassabre and poor visibility prevented air support. Theaim of the us rhird Army offensive was to crear the southern partof the saar valley and eventually to estabrish a bridgehead overthe Rhine. rn this operation, the us 4th Armored Division (271tanks) was opposed by 9 German divisions, 2 brigades, and somemiscellaneous units (I45 tanks). The German Mark IVs, Mark Vs,and Mark vrs were superior to the us M4s in frotation, gunpower,and armor protectin. Despite their smaller force, the Germansfought a successful delaying action. They suited their tactics totake full advantage of the geographic and climatic conditions, while--because of these cir.cumstances--the United States was engaged inan offensive for which tanks with marginal flotation were not wellsuited. rncruding 10 tanks that were recovered and returned toservj-ce, the us rosses were 55 tanks to the German loss of 6t. ?husarthough the Germans were outnumbered in tank strength almost 2:r,tank losses were almost equar for the two sides. Ten us M5s and45 miscellaneous German tanks that wer.e present were not suitablefor action against opposing arrnor and antitank weapons, and if they
