St. Vith, I7-23 December 1944The mission of CCB of the 7th Armored Division at St. Vithwas to hold at all costs in order to disrupt the German Ardennesoffensive. Through use of excellent defensive tactics it didhold for six days against greatty supenior German forces, delayingthe Germans long enough to make certain the fail-ure of the Germanoffensive. Inadequate German planning allowed the German offensivespearheads to be held up by traffic jam, so tlat they reached St.Vith after the 7th Armored. The Germans committed their tankspiecemeal for three days, wasting the advantage of their numbers.CCB used a mobile defense, defending dominant terrain with dug-ininfantry and tank destroyers, while holding its tanks as a mobilecounterattacking force. Repeated US spoiling attacks kept theGermans off balance and made them believe they faced a much largerforce than they did. The mobility of the M4 tank permitted thesuccessful implementation of these tactics. l[oss ratios to beincluded when charts are compl-eted./Bastogne, 17-20 December 1944In the area east of Bastogrne, in southeastenn Belgium, on theedge of the Ardennes Forest, el-ements of two American armoreddivisions, the 9th and the 10th, clashed with elements of th::ee
