OFFICIAL USE ONLY
A1am Halfa, 31 Auqust-3 September 1942Elements of the British 7th and 10th Armored Divisions con-ducted a successful defense of AIam Halfa in the Egyptian WesternDesert against General Erwin Rommelrs Afrika Corps (I5th and 2lstPanzer Divisions) by using their tanks dug in, in a static defen-sive role. This unconventional use of armor was dictated by thequalitative inferiority of the Grant medium tanks with which theBritish were equipped. The Grants had 75mm. short guns (31 cal.long) with relatively l..ow muzzle velocity, and speed not signifi-cantly higher than that of the opposing Pz.Kpfw. IIIs and IVs,which were much more heavily gunned (50mm., L/60 and 75mm.,L/43or L/48). The usefulness of the Grant gun was further severelyIimited by its low side mount, which penrnitted little adjustmentof elevation or traverse. The Grants ? high silhouettes also madethem easy targets on the battlefield and, as the British used them,the tank commander had to partly expose himself in order to directfire.
A breakthrough at AIam Halfa was essential to Rommel, as hecould no }onger support himself in Egypt unless he could breakthrough to Alexandria and Cairo. Thus, although the numericallysuperior British force lost tanks in a ratio of L.34:L, Rommelrsnepulse was not only a British tactical success, but also a signi-ficant strategic victory.
