OFFICIAL USE ONLY
An important factor in the battle was a British minefieldthat delayed the Ger:mans for six hours, forcing Rommel to attackin daylight and l-ose the advantage of surprise, and also so de-pleting his limited fuel supplies that he was forced to attackfrontally, rather than make the flanking sweep he had planned.British air harassment also contributed sigrnificantly to thevictory.Chouigui Pass--26 November 1942Tank speed and maneuverability, together with a near-perfecttactical plan, were the main factors influencing the small-unitUS victory at Chouigui Pass in Tunisia, the first US armor actionof World War II. In over,aII qualitative characteristics, theGerman tanks outclassed the American tanks. A detachment of theGerman l90th Panzer Battalion, 10th Panzer Division, was fightingwith 13 Mark IIf and Mark IV medium tanks, all of which wereequipped with 50mm. and 75mm. guns. The US force--two companiesof the lst Battalion, Ist Armored Regiment, had 25 tanks, alllight M3s, wj.th 37mm. guns, 53 calibers long that were completelyincapable of penetrating the German al:mor. The mobility of thelight tanks, coupled with superior tactics, enabled the Americansto hit the German tanks in the rean, engines, and tracks, resultingin a US loss of only 28% of the tanks engaged, and a German loss of69%. The US to German loss ratio was 1:1.29.
