OFFICIAL USE ONLY
2. Numbers, in general, are more important than firepoweror protection, because of the advantageS superior numbers providein maneuver and nass. Numben, however, are evidently less im-portant than mobility, unlesS the numerical inferiority is over-whelming.3, In engagements of large numbers of adequate tanks, theside with the greatest mobility is usually victorious, unless itis greatly outnumbered, on unless its tanks are seriously infen-ior in one of the three major operational characteristics. Thetulning point in terrns of the relationship of mobility to numbersdepends on too many factors to be specified precisely, but tte ef-fects of disparity of numbers are obviously reduced by effectivedoctrine, adequate training, and good leadership on the side withnumerical inferiority.4. The effects of numerical inferiority of tanks aregreatty minimized by the availability of supporting forces ofother arms, particularly antitank weaPons, artillery, infantry,and air Support. These supporting weaPons and units have theprirnary effect of enhancing the mobility of friendly tanks, andinhibiting the mobility of the opposition.Not surprisingly, this study shows that, all other thingsbeing equal:a. Superio:: leadersLuip is usually suceessful.b. Superior numbers usuallY win.c. Superior weapons usually defeat inferior.
endstream
