The study also suggests certain conclusions with respect to variousmixes of leadership, number:s, and weapons. If all the variablesand intangible factors of combat (leadenship, terrain, morale,availability of supporting troops, etc.) ane equal, and if thesize of the forces is appnoximately equal, tanks with superiorcharacteristics will ordinarily defeat an equal number of tankswith lesser characteristics. If the tanks of one side have asuperionity i-n one or more characteristics, while those of theother side have supeniority in other character,istics, the outcomeof a conflict between equal numbers is much harder to predict.The results of engagements studied would suggest, however, thatif the tanks on both sides rneet at least minimum operational re-quirements fon adequacy in all five operational characteni-stics,then the listed order of prionity of the five characteriscics(mobility, firepower, protection, endurance, and reliability)become particularly significant. There shourd be exploration ofthe possibifity of assigning relative quantified values to thesecharacteristics, for comparative purposes.It ls understood that the purpose of this study is to as-sist policymakers in deciding an allocation of available resourcesto obtain the maximum return for armored conflict. Thus, it isessentj-al at the outset to determine how and why tanks can con-tribute to success in battle. There see to be three major pos-sible roles for tanks, not mutually exclusive, and not necessa::ily
