in order of relatlve importance: (f) to defeat other tanks1' (2)to provide the most economical collection of protected firepowerfor defensel (3) to contribute to maximum overall combat capabil-ity in the maximum possible variety of combat situations.It is undeniable that the best antitank weapon is a tank--but it is an extremely expensive weapon. The resources of men,complicated manufactured materials, and money required for produc-tion, tr,aining, and deployment could produce a number of high-velocity antitank guns, tank mines, and the highly mobile troopsrequired to man these guns and place these mines, which would insum be rnore effective in antitank defense than wourd the singretank they reprace. Thus, if our mission is simpry to defeat othertanks, the best allocation of resources might welr be to antitankmeans other than tanks.It is clear, however, that an enerny tank force meeting sucha defense of antitank guns and mines would employ a combined armsapproach in which infantry, artillery, and engineers, supported bytanks, would be employed.To counter such a combined arms offensive against our anti-tank defenses, it would be essential to have, in addition to thepurely antitank defense, adequate quantities of defending infantryand artillery, plus, probably, a small number of very powenfultanks, particularly strong in fir:epower and protection to workwith infantry and artillery in opposing hostile attacks and to
