appears to have been amply demonstrated that the shock action andexploitation capabilities of tanks against Llnarrored forces areenhanced by numbers, probably in geometric proportion, in termsboth of psychological impact and of maneuver capabilities.It is probably irnpossible to come up with a forrnula whichwill express the ideal relationship between numbers of tanks andquantities of unarmored units armed with small arms as well aswith heavier weapons, including artillery. In the type of rela-tively open terrain which favors any use of armored forces inIange numbers, however, it is probable that a pnoportion of onearmored division to each other type of division would not be toornuch armor. Since it is doubtful that any nation will have theresources to cover vast fronts with this high proportion of armorto infantry and other arms, the ratio could be considered as anoptimum objectlve. It would thus appear that resounces availablefor armor along should be so allocated as to produce maximum num-ber,s of tanks that are adequate in the four lower priority char-acteristics, and that are highly mobile under all likely combatcircumstances. It would appear to be a serious rnistake to try toapproach the optimum in all five characteristics, if this were tomean a substantially smaller number of tanks.An irnportant factor in the decision must be the realizationthat the role of armored forces cannot and should not be lirniteidto combat with opposing armor. We have noted that nonarmored
