t
OFFICIAL USE ONLY
antitank defenses can be made far stronger than would be possibleby allocating the same nesounces to armor for defensive purposes.l4ore significant, however, is the demonstrated fact of two moreimportant, and overriding roles for armor: (1) extensive, rapid,and relatively secure maneuver capabiU-ty in intensive combatl(2) the psychological effect of the employrnent of tanks in massagainst hostile unarmored forces; and (3) breakthrough and exploi-tation in rear areas with r,elative imperviousness to rear areastroops and countermeasures. Thus in allocating resources the aimshould be a combination of highly mobile, but othetwise merelyadequate, armor in combinatlon with effective antitank weapons,artillery, and infantny, to be employed both offensively and de-fensively (bearing in mind that firepower j-s the most impontantoperational characteristic of armor in combined arms defense).But it should be further borne in mind that the primary role ofthe t-ank is offensive, rather than defensive, and that other,nonarmoued measures are available to offset tank deficiencies innumbers and quality in the defense.On balance, then, the effectiveness of tanks in their primarymissions of maneuver and shock action is at least directly propor-tional to the number of t'adequaterf tanks available. This wouldseem to justify a production policy which will assure the maximumnumber of tanks which at least rneet all five of the minimum opera-tional requirements, and which exceed the minimum mobilityrequi.rement.
