Limitations on Data aLd on AnalvgisFor reasons discussed eanlier in this report (Section I,Methodologv), it has been necessary to proceed as rapidly aspossible to the analysis of as many individual tank engagementsfon which sufficient data b,as available to permit a reasonablyvalid analysis of armored conflict. While every possible effontwas rnade to find actions representative of as wide a variety oftank engagements as possible, the initial approach was necessarilyin the nature of a net-casti-ng process, rather than a deliberateapproach to analysis of the results of specific actions, or seriesof actions, as part of a planned and systernatic procedure. Fur'-thermore, Iack of available data for some actlons or campaignswhich had been on the original list has led still further towarda collection of individual studies selected fon convenience,rather than as elernents of a sound, systems apProach.The engagements studied ane for the rnost part those forwhich primary and secondary source material is readily availablebecause the actions wene in themselves dramatic on unusual, onwere parts of largen, dramatic operations. Not unexpectedly,these are more often than not aetions in which a high orden ofIeadership, or special circumstances, resulted in victory by tank
