OFFICIAL USE ONLY
units that were outnumbered, or were outgunned, or that other-wise suffered under some serious disadvantage. Fortunately,there are enough of these actions, ln different armies, to pro-vide some sort of balance, but this balance is largely fortuitous.It is not clear from our data whether the actions we describewere typical, or quite atypical, or something in between. Wesuspect the latter, but we cannot be certain frorn the data thatwe have.This does not in any way invalidate the data, analysis, orthe findings, of any of the individual actions investigated inthis study, nor--we believe--does it invalidate the overall con-clusions we shall draw from review of these findings in the nextsection of this report. But it does mean that we cannot be cer-tain that we have all of the conclusions which might be desinablefor the purposes of this study.What is particularly lacking from this study is an lndicationof the effect of sustained operations on anmored conflict, and ondifferent kinds of tanks with vanying characteristics, panticularlyin the kind of war of rapid and long-range movement for whicharmor is particularly suited. In the time available for thisstudy there was not sufficient time nor funds to undertake thepainstaking collection of basic primary source data on the opexa-tions of one on more units over extended per,iods of time, to in-clude all- of the opposi-ng force data essential to the analysis.
