Appendix CeoMMENTS OS GERMAN r.fir,ITARy pOCqMENTS(A Report by Wlodzimierz Onacewicz)I. Examined microfilms of German military documents at theNational Archives contain a tremendous amount of unpublished in-formation on various aspects of the Gerrnan military establishmentduring World War II, its organization and armament, strategy andtactics, and descriptions and analyses of battles and campaigns,evaluations of the enemy, and evidence of shortcomings and diffi-culties experienced by the Gerrnans in the last years of the war.This informat j-on is contained in war diar.ies of divisions, armycorps, armies, and army groups; combat and logistic orders andreports; intelligence documents I reports on human and materiallosses and replacements; analyses of battte experience; and a greatvariety of other docurnents. It must be observed that because ofthe unsystematic way in which the microfilmed files wene cornpiled,the search for a definite kind of information requires examinationof a considerable number of unrelated documents and is an extremelytime-consuming process. However, we have learned that the resultsof such search are often rewanding.
