2. Our recent specifie search for information on Gerrnanmilitary losses, human and material, particularly for armoredconflict, in most cases gave only limited nesults. This was duepantially to limited tirne available for search, but mainly tothe scarcity of reports on losses in the files. Our researchproved beyond any doubt that in the German armed forces daily andperiodical reports on human and material losses were systematicallycompiled by aIl German units and headquarters, and forwarded tothe next level of command. When we found such reports (usuallyprepared on special printed forms), they proved excellent, de-tailed, complete, and apparently accurate, and were most usefulto our studies. Trying to establish the cause of scarcity oflosses reports, in the rnicrof ilrned f iles, we learned that a greatnumber of German military files have been returned to Germanywithout being micr,ofilrned, which suggests that among nonmicrofilmedand returned documents were reports on losses that, because theywere so common, did not seem important to the persons who selecteddocuments for microfilming. Thus it seems most probable that themissing reports on losses eould be located in Germany.
3. 9ogcLqs:lon. Exploration of German military documents,
as a primary source of lnfonmation on the German side in WorldWar II, is of great significance to our studies on the postwardevelopment of various aspects of military doctrine, principlesof modern combat, and combat effectiveness of modern weapons. In
